<LegalPage title="Artist Terms" kicker="For artists">
<p class="lede">Here’s the deal, in plain English. You make the work. We help people find it and go stand in front of it. These terms are what we’re each agreeing to when you put your work on Artists Are Jerks.</p>

<h3>Who can join</h3>
<ul>
  <li>You’re at least 18 years old.</li>
  <li>You make art that fits our <a href="/guidelines/">Community Guidelines</a>.</li>
  <li>A real person reads every request. We can say no. We’ll usually tell you why, but we don’t always have to.</li>
  <li>One account, one artist page. Keep your sign-in to yourself.</li>
</ul>

<h3>Your work stays yours</h3>
<p>You keep the copyright and every other right in your work. Joining doesn’t transfer any of it to us.</p>
<p>To put your work on the site, you give us permission to store, copy, resize, and display the photos and words you add. That covers the site itself, the emails it sends (like the note your followers get when you have a new showing), and the small preview of your page that appears when someone shares a link to it in a post or a text. The permission is free, it isn’t exclusive (you can show your work anywhere else you like), and it’s only for running the site and pointing people to your work. The site won’t post your work on its own social media accounts or in ads without asking you first.</p>
<p>That permission ends when you remove a piece or close your page. Two exceptions: copies in our backups, which cycle out within 30 days, and emails that already went out.</p>

<h3>What you promise us</h3>
<ul>
  <li><strong>It’s your work.</strong> You made it, or you have the right to show it. The photos are yours, or you have permission to use them.</li>
  <li><strong>You have the permissions the work needs.</strong> If a piece shows a recognizable person, making sure it’s OK to show it publicly is your responsibility.</li>
  <li><strong>AI is labeled.</strong> If AI generated the image, or a real part of it, you check “Made with AI.” Ordinary photo editing on a picture of your physical work, like cropping or color correction, doesn’t count.</li>
  <li><strong>Showings are real.</strong> When you list a showing, the work is on that wall (or will be) on those dates. You’re vouching for the place. For ongoing showings, you answer the “Still up?” check-in.</li>
  <li><strong>Your details are accurate</strong> enough for people to find you and your work.</li>
</ul>

<h3>Selling your work</h3>
<p>We don’t sell anything, handle money, or take a commission. If someone wants to buy a piece, that’s between you and them. The prices on your page are yours to set. If a price changes or a piece sells, update it.</p>

<h3>People who reach you</h3>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Messages.</strong> If you turn on the message form, visitors write to you through the site and we forward it to your email. Your address stays hidden until you reply. Replying shows them your address, the same as any email.</li>
  <li><strong>Followers.</strong> These are people who asked to hear when you have work up somewhere. You can see the list and download it. Use it only to tell those people about your own art and showings. Don’t sell it or hand it to anyone else. If you email them yourself, give them an easy way to stop hearing from you, and honor it. The site’s own notices always include an unsubscribe link.</li>
</ul>

<h3>What we can do</h3>
<ul>
  <li>Hide a piece, a showing, or your whole page if it breaks these terms or the Guidelines, or if someone credibly says it’s theirs. We’ll email you when we do, usually with the reason, and you can always write to us about it.</li>
  <li>Close an account for serious or repeated problems, including the three-strikes rule on our <a href="/copyright/">Copyright</a> page.</li>
  <li>Change, pause, or shut down the site. If we shut it down for good, you’ll get 30 days’ notice.</li>
</ul>

<h3>Leaving</h3>
<p>You can take your page offline anytime from the “Your page” tab. To close your account entirely, write to us. Within 30 days, we’ll delete your page, pieces, showings, messages, and follower list.</p>

<h3>What it costs</h3>
<p>Nothing, right now. If that changes, we’ll tell you at least 60 days ahead, and you won’t be charged anything you haven’t agreed to.</p>

<h3>The legal part, still in plain English</h3>
<ul>
  <li>The site comes as it is. We work hard on it, but we can’t promise it will always be up, free of mistakes, or bring you visitors or buyers.</li>
  <li>We’re not part of what happens between you and a venue, a buyer, or a visitor. That includes sales and payments, and it includes loans of work, delivery, and any damage or loss to your work while it’s hanging somewhere.</li>
  <li>As far as the law allows, if something goes wrong, our total responsibility to you is limited to $100.</li>
  <li>If something you post gets the site into legal trouble, like a piece that turns out not to be yours, that’s your responsibility, not the site’s.</li>
  <li>Oregon law applies. Disputes go to the courts in Jackson County, Oregon.</li>
</ul>

<h3>When these terms change</h3>
<p>We’ll email you and show you the new version the next time you sign in. You’ll be asked to agree again before you can keep publishing. The version date is always at the top.</p>

<h3>Talking to us</h3>
<p>Use the “Write to us” link at the top of your workspace, or email info@artistsarejerks.com.</p>
</LegalPage>
